# TODO - Android and browser packaging

## Objective

Support Android ARM64 and browser targets where compatible.

## Acceptance

- Use official Android and WASM toolchains on supported hosts.
- Test touch, storage, graphics capabilities and packaging.
- Report unavailable targets rather than claiming support.

## Evidence

Pending. Record actual checks and remaining limitations before completion.

Install the official Rust standard-library targets for the selected stable
toolchain. Cross-target library checks are mandatory in the shared
validator:

```sh
rustup target add aarch64-linux-android wasm32-unknown-unknown \
  aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu \
  aarch64-pc-windows-gnullvm x86_64-pc-windows-gnu
cargo validate --no-cache
```

This checks every workspace library under crates without default features.
New title packages enter the matrix automatically. The validation cache
includes the installed target inventory. It does not link an Android
application, produce a browser module, or execute either target.

On GNU/Linux with Rust 1.100.0, both official target components installed
and all three libraries passed cross-target checking in fresh validation
run 1795614497436019870-1973701. The Android SDK/NDK and browser packaging
and runtime adapters remain unavailable or unimplemented. No device,
WebAssembly module, graphics, touch or storage acceptance is claimed.

The Android ARM64 opt-in check now includes the native storage adapter
alongside the three portable libraries. Fresh validation run
1795619165836589916-3124876 compiled the storage library for Android
and repeated the WebAssembly library checks. Android atomic package
publication remains GNU/Linux-specific; no Android link, device storage,
runtime or package acceptance is inferred from cross-target checking.

The optional gate now also checks the importer and storage facade for
all three targets, including Windows GNU x86-64. Fresh validation run
1795637347765536328-1840915 passed all five library checks on Android,
WebAssembly and Windows. The storage facade reports unsupported on
WebAssembly and Windows rather than attempting a weaker file open.
This is compile-time evidence only; no target was linked or executed.

On 2026-11-25 the official Android ARM64 and WebAssembly Rust targets
remained installed, and the mandatory six-target portable core gate
passed. Android Debug Bridge was installed, but no SDK or NDK root was
configured or found in the conventional local locations. The browser
and Node.js were installed, but no authored browser runtime or packaging
adapter exists. A standard-library target or a browser alone is not a
working distribution toolchain. The title build command now distinguishes
recognized core-only targets from invalid target names and refuses each
of the five non-native distribution requests before invoking Cargo.
Android and browser packaging, runtime graphics, input and storage
acceptance remain open.

Fresh validation run 1795675217834953245-2363038 linked the first
authored portable gameplay smoke using the official WebAssembly target
and Rust 1.100.0, without the native preview features. The optimized
75,597-byte module ran in Node.js 24.18.0 with an empty host import
object and returned success. The same scenario is exercised by native
tests. It verifies all four players join, move 16 world units over
eight fixed ticks, stop on release, enter and leave the pause phase, and
reach identical state across one-frame and sixteen-frame scheduling.
The shared validator now requires this executable check, fingerprints
the Node.js version and options, and still checks all six portable
library targets. It does not require original media.

The headless Firefox attempt in a network-isolated local sandbox failed
during browser startup because the sandbox did not permit access to
/dev/null. No browser execution or graphics, touch or storage acceptance
is inferred. Android linking, device execution and distribution remain
open; the title still cannot be packaged for Android or browsers.

Follow-up headless Firefox 160.0 execution succeeded after supplying an
isolated device filesystem and process filesystem to the sandbox. The
sandbox had no network access and used a fresh local profile. The HTML
embedded exactly the optimized 75,597-byte smoke module; SHA-256 of
the decoded bytes matched the linked module. The successful main return
produced a green screenshot (240 by 160; centre pixel 0,255,0).
A negative-control page calling a nonexistent export produced a red
screenshot (centre pixel 255,0,0). This proves the same asset-free
simulation smoke executed in a browser engine, not that the title has
browser rendering, audio, input, persistent storage or packaging support.

The existing featureless title executable now also exercises the shared
engine's RGBA row bounds, uncropped bottom-panel presentation, landscape
and portrait touch projection, bounded contact admission and cancellation,
and portable display-preference save encoding. It rejects an out-of-row
and overflowing pixel request, contacts in the panel bars, duplicate or
excess contacts, stale motion after cancellation and corrupt or oversized
save payloads. It reuses the native engine contracts without a browser
adapter, original assets or a new runtime dependency.

Fresh validation 1795675823751135383-2460781 passed 1,859 aggregate
test executions, all six portable library targets and the extended
WebAssembly executable. The resulting optimized module was 168,140
