The official wasm-bindgen release is now a fixed reference catalogue
entry pinned to the existing 0.2.127 binding ABI. The latest 0.2.130
release introduces a second major version of syn and was rejected by
the unchanged dependency gate. The matching Linux CLI archive was
fetched with `cargo download wasm-bindgen-release` and verified against
both the upstream checksum file and release asset SHA-256
61d4a7dc85acfa0d2354ccc0b8361928c7e52a746d17f28ebaa795ed3dc1614a.
The CLI is not installed or executed automatically by the downloader.

The WebAssembly save-slot adapter now uses browser local storage with
a dedicated 1-to-64-byte ASCII namespace and numeric slots. It bounds
the raw JavaScript string before transferring it into Rust and rejects
noncanonical text, malformed envelopes and excess sizes using the
shared portable save contract. Each write replaces one origin-local
value. Successful writes report unsupported crash durability instead
of a filesystem sync guarantee; quota, disabled storage, browser
eviction and same-origin writers remain limitations.

Fresh validation 1795676590731272457-2618033 passed 1,864 aggregate
test executions, all six portable library targets, the Node.js core
smoke and linking a separate opt-in browser storage diagnostic. The
raw storage module was 589,394 bytes; the official 0.2.127 CLI
produced 76,269 bytes of WebAssembly and 11,010 bytes of browser
JavaScript. The generated module SHA-256 was
8b9a4fa537b2db32e3656f860b3e652503a0cb00482608e2795b9ebe72c7392a,
matching the module embedded in the local diagnostic page.

Firefox 160.0 ran the storage diagnostic over a loopback HTTP origin
within an isolated network namespace and a fresh private profile.
Six independent browser launches verified first publication, reload,
checksum damage, oversized text, noncanonical uppercase text and
restoration. The harness independently computed the exact portable
envelopes, observed storage both before and after binding startup
(which invokes main), and checked a second explicit main return.
Successful cases returned zero and negative cases returned one without
replacing the rejected data. All six screenshot indicators were green.
The initial first-launch indicator was red because the harness only
observed the second invocation; the corrected harness observed both.
The executable also verified numeric slot and namespace separation,
publication of binary payloads, rejection of oversized writes and
the explicit unsupported durability receipt.

This establishes a real browser persistence slice, not browser title
packaging, physical touch, graphics presentation, audio, portable save
transfer between origins or crash-durability acceptance. Android
SDK/NDK and attached device remain unavailable.

The browser host now exposes the document's monotonic performance clock
through the shared measurement capability. It converts fractional
milliseconds into durations, rejects invalid or unrepresentable
timestamps and retains the portable reversed-interval rejection. Clock
origins must not be compared across documents, workers or processes.
Browser resident memory and executable size remain unsupported.

Fresh validation 1795677054225325622-2687397 passed 1,869 aggregate
test executions and the six-target matrix. The browser diagnostic
now observes the clock before opening storage. The official binding CLI
generated a 77,688-byte module and 11,348-byte JavaScript binding.
The exact module embedded in the diagnostic had SHA-256
f9761f1e2a70947ed9d604a9ccd2ee89d66eaaa89825dcabf72cf2019935ea3d.

Eight isolated Firefox 160.0 launches passed. The six storage cases
also observed four finite, nonnegative, nondecreasing timestamps from
the actual browser clock per launch. Equal timestamps were accepted
at browser timer precision. Two injected host-clock controls returned
failure before changing the stored value: a NaN timestamp and a
100-to-99-millisecond reversed interval. All eight screenshots
reported green acceptance indicators. This establishes a browser
clock observation adapter, not a browser game loop, graphics,
touch or distribution acceptance.

Additional isolated Firefox host-failure acceptance used the same
clock-and-storage diagnostic module. After creating a valid slot, three
independent launches injected a storage getter SecurityError, an absent
storage object, and a setItem QuotaExceededError. Each returned failure
on both startup and explicit invocation without replacing the valid
slot. A subsequent unmodified reload succeeded. A separate fresh
profile with Firefox's dom.storage.enabled preference set to false
returned no local storage object, and the diagnostic returned failure.
All six acceptance indicators were green. The injected exception tests
do not measure a real browser quota or establish crash durability.
